Compare alternatives under constraints and explicit tradeoffs (@engineering-alternatives: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Connect-realization-to-verification-and-intended-use.lingua#r_0C593F4A7A204FABB3B5876653") { r_0848D9CBAED01BC33DD0081EA7
A design decision needs alternatives that achieve the same intended function. First ask which candidates violate a necessary condition. Then compare the remaining candidates using quantities that matter for the stated use. A preferred technology, a single attractive number or a detailed drawing cannot replace this comparison.

**Read:** [NASA Systems Engineering Handbook](../mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/sources/Systems-Engineering-Handbook-NASA.lingua#r_7ZQFZ9E1NAVG27SE2R5S24PDBQ) section 4.4, connecting candidate solutions to the requirements already read. Revisit [Slocum topic 1](../mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/sources/FUNdaMENTALS-of-Design-Slocum.lingua#r_58Q6VQJ3FXXGXRBJ3DK0AVC3WQ)'s opening discussion of the progression from concepts toward details. Generate alternatives before committing to detailed component calculations. A manual arrangement, changed work sequence and altered product interface can be meaningful alternatives alongside different machines.

The following numbers are invented teaching data. Output is only an aggregate upper bound; area and energy describe the same assumed ten-hour accounting interval and include the same declared equipment boundary.

| Candidate | Area, m² | Energy, kWh | Acceptable-output upper bound in ten hours |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| A | 4 | 6 | 931 |
| B | 6 | 4 | 1,005.48 |
| C | 5 | 7 | 900 |
| D | 3 | 3 | 760 |

For a requirement of at least 800 accepted cards, D fails even the relaxed capacity screen. Passing that screen does not prove A, B or C can actually deliver 800: timing and product evidence remain missing. Among these listed attributes, A is no worse than C and is strictly better on each, so C is dominated. A and B trade area against energy and output capacity. A is preferable under a 5 m² space limit; B remains a candidate when the limit is 7 m² and energy use has greater importance. No claim is made that any real device has these properties.

The comparison changes if maintainability, acquisition resources, noise, working conditions, repair time or another important attribute differs. Dominance established on an incomplete table is conditional on that table. A weighted score also needs justified scales: adding square metres directly to kilowatt-hours is meaningless. Normalize quantities against explicit references, explain the preference weights, and test whether a plausible change of weight reverses the result. Keeping several non-dominated candidates can be more honest than inventing a precise universal score.

Sensitivity asks how a conclusion responds to changed inputs. In the previous line model, increasing r_A from 120 to 200/h leaves the final upper bound at 931 while B remains limiting. Increasing r_B to 110/h changes its input capacity to 1,045, so A's 1,026 becomes limiting and the final bound is 1,005.48. This is an aggregate relaxation; an observed accepted count must be integral. Neither change establishes a real improvement until its effects on yield, availability, resources and interfaces are examined.

**Work:** reject D by a necessary condition, show A's dominance over C with componentwise inequalities, and state why A and B cannot be ordered without further preferences or constraints. Add one invented maintenance attribute that removes C's dominance, clearly labeling the new assumption. Distinguish a candidate worth investigating from a verified working design. Propose the next observation most likely to change your choice.

**Continue:** [Connect realization to verification and intended use](Connect-realization-to-verification-and-intended-use.lingua#r_0C593F4A7A204FABB3B5876653).

